    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.29121/shodhgyan.v4.i1.2026.94",
    "abstract": "This research paper analyzes the gradual decline of the Indus Valley Civilization (IVC), one of the most advanced urban-centered civilizations of the ancient world, and the crucial role of climate change in its demise. For a long time, it was believed that the civilization ended due to foreign invasions or sudden floods, but modern archaeological evidence and paleoclimate data tell a different story.The main focus of this study is the weakening of the South-West Monsoon around 1900 BCE and the resulting prolonged aridity. This paper examines how the changes in monsoon patterns affected river levels, particularly impacting the Ghaggar-Hakra (Saraswati) river system. The water scarcity not only eliminated agricultural surpluses but also disrupted the food supply chains of the large cities.In conclusion, this research establishes that the decline of the Indus Valley Civilization was not a sudden event but a cultural transformation, where adverse climatic conditions forced people to abandon organized urban life and migrate eastward (to the Ganges plains) and southward in the form of smaller rural communities. This study also offers a historical warning and lesson for the current global climate crisis.",
    "field": "Environmental archaeology",
    "problem": "Prevailing historical theories attributing the Harappan decline to singular events like invasion or floods are inadequate, failing to consider modern scientific evidence of a gradual collapse driven by prolonged monsoon failure and resulting aridity.",
    "method": "This study employs a multi-disciplinary approach, integrating archaeological evidence with paleo-climate data, remote sensing imagery, and comparative chronology to analyze the link between climatic factors and the decline of the Indus Valley Civilization.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze the decline of the Harappan Civilization, arguing that environmental and climatic factors, particularly monsoon weakening and changes in river systems, were the primary causes for its cultural transformation.",
